J. A ripple at two radii. The equation says the part with m waves at an inner radius is the same part at an outer one, shrunk by (inner/outer)^(m^2/3), plus what was gathered between:
   so its two amplitudes (the cos and the sin) are each correlated across clouds by (inner/outer)^(m^2/3) x sqrt(g_m(outer) / g_m(inner)), with no constant in it. Measured (standard error by resampling the clouds), predicted for two single radii, and predicted for two annuli of the width used:
   exactly n =   100000, 1 wave:  0.8 and 0.3: 0.441 (0.008), 0.431, 0.435;  0.9 and 0.5: 0.410 (0.010), 0.390, 0.397;  0.6 and 0.2: 0.568 (0.007), 0.563, 0.568;  0.5 and 0.4: 0.877 (0.003), 0.870, 0.876
   exactly n =   100000, 2 waves:  0.8 and 0.3: 0.238 (0.011), 0.236, 0.240;  0.9 and 0.5: 0.298 (0.011), 0.280, 0.286;  0.6 and 0.2: 0.278 (0.011), 0.272, 0.278;  0.5 and 0.4: 0.767 (0.005), 0.756, 0.767
   exactly n =   100000, 3 waves:  0.8 and 0.3: 0.075 (0.012), 0.061, 0.063;  0.9 and 0.5: 0.144 (0.011), 0.138, 0.143;  0.6 and 0.2: 0.057 (0.011), 0.052, 0.054;  0.5 and 0.4: 0.559 (0.009), 0.547, 0.565
   exactly n =  1000000, 1 wave:  0.8 and 0.3: 0.436 (0.013), 0.431, 0.435;  0.9 and 0.5: 0.391 (0.013), 0.390, 0.397;  0.6 and 0.2: 0.567 (0.010), 0.563, 0.568;  0.5 and 0.4: 0.877 (0.004), 0.870, 0.876
